1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:00,460
Today,

2
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:03,460
um, we are putting on X-ray glasses.

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:12,540
We're looking at a model country called Progressa to see the hidden blueprint that keeps massive bureaucracies functioning at scale.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:19,450
Yeah, because to really understand a system of that size, you have to look past all the endless departments and portals, you know?

5
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:20,700
Right. It's too much otherwise.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,020
Exactly. You have to map its distinct layers.

7
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:28,420
Mm. It is really the only way to see what is actually working and where efforts are quietly duplicated.

8
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:34,000
Well, we can get a head start by breaking Progressa's education sector down into five key bodies.

9
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:35,099
Yeah, five bodies.

10
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:41,280
So first, there's the Ministry of Education setting the rules, then the National Examination Authority, which is actually delivering the services.

11
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:45,690
Uh-huh. Then you have the Learner Register acting as the single source for who is a student,

12
00:00:46,310 --> 00:00:53,720
the National Identity Authority, which owns the person identity, and finally, the Digital Government Authority running the data exchange.

13
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:57,080
And I mean, classifying them this way isn't just arbitrary.

14
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:01,060
It establishes the strict boundaries of their authority, which prevents overlap.

15
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:03,880
It kind of feels like, um, like a franchise model.

16
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:04,660
Oh, how so?

17
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,900
Like the Ministry is corporate, right. They set the menu.

18
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,610
The Examination Authority is the local branch actually serving the customer.

19
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:15,450
Right. Right. But the Digital Government and Identity Authorities,

20
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:20,560
those are the central supply chains. The local branch can bake the cake,

21
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:22,960
but they aren't allowed to farm their own wheat.

22
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,820
That is exactly it, and they cannot farm their own wheat because of how the business layer operates.

23
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:31,840
This is where we separate a service from a capability.

24
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:36,000
Wait, I'm a little lost there. What is the difference between a service and a capability?

25
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:38,200
I mean, aren't they basically the same thing?

26
00:01:38,550 --> 00:01:47,000
Not quite. So a service is the outcome you actually experience as a citizen, like transferring schools or, uh, receiving a diploma.

27
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:53,390
Okay. But a capability is the specific mechanical function required to make that happen, like the act of registering a learner.

28
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:58,500
A service might touch several departments, but a capability is owned by exactly one body.

29
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:02,300
Oh, I see. So registering a learner belongs exclusively to the Learner Register.

30
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:04,560
Exactly, and that ties directly into the data layer.

31
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:10,930
The core idea here is the once only principle, meaning, um, one data domain has one owner.

32
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:13,640
So the Identity Authority owns the person domain.

33
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:17,520
Yes, and no one else is allowed to mint a new identity.

34
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,240
The Examination Authority just consumes it.

35
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:23,160
Got it. So if the Examination Authority needs to verify a student,

36
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:27,380
they just query the Identity Authority. They don't create a new record.

37
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:32,940
Precisely. But honestly, I mean, wouldn't it just be faster for the Examination Authority to keep its own list?

38
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:39,180
Why go through the bureaucratic hassle of pinging another department every single time a student sits for a test?

39
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:43,079
Well, it feels faster on day one, but it is a structural trap.

40
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:47,980
If the Examination Authority maintains its own list, you have just created a duplicate register.

41
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:50,320
Oh, right. Because then you have two lists.

42
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:56,790
Exactly. Systems become siloed. When a student moves, their address gets updated in one system but not the other.

43
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:03,140
Information is constantly rekeyed, and the entire infrastructure becomes incredibly costly to change.

44
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:11,940
Yeah, that makes sense. So if the Identity Authority holds the definitive data, the actual software, like the apps citizens and bureaucrats use,

45
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:14,780
m-must have to plug into it somehow to pull that info.

46
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:16,799
And that brings us to the application layer.

47
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:19,680
Software maps to specific capabilities.

48
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:25,340
So an exam management application does not store its own identity data.

49
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:26,760
It just pulls it from elsewhere.

50
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:36,600
Right. It points up to the capability of running an exam, and it points across to the data domains it needs, retrieving data from the learner and person domains.

51
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:41,740
So if the Identity Authority updates its database- Mm-hmm... you don't have to rewrite the exam application.

52
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:44,680
Yep. The app just relies on the technology layer underneath.

53
00:03:44,970 --> 00:03:48,100
Okay. You know, the shared data exchange backbones keeping everything connected.

54
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:52,780
Exactly. And tracing one service top to bottom really reveals the structure.

55
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:58,580
Say a citizen sits an exam. Right. That relies on the Examination Authority's capability to run that exam.

56
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:02,560
That capability uses an exam management application.

57
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:06,760
The application draws on three data domains: learner, person, and result.

58
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,840
And it all sits on those shared technology platforms.

59
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:12,580
Exactly. It's all one cohesive building block structure.

60
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:19,240
It is wild when you take the sheer scale of government operations and reveal the clean, logical machinery underneath.

61
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:21,899
It really changes how you look at organizations.

62
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:22,330
It does,

